25, 0.0002658367156982422, 0.04803466796875, -0.016815185546875, -0.0032634735107421875, -0.01763916015625, 0.01230621337890625, 0.033447265625, 0.040313720703125, -0.035369873046875, 0.0044708251953125, -0.01374053955078125, -0.0162811279296875, 0.0023651123046875, 0.008941650390625, -0.005962371826171875, -0.02667236328125, 0.003997802734375, 0.02667236328125, -0.0131072998046875, 0.00485992431640625, -0.023223876953125, -0.050537109375, 0.0150299072265625, 0.0284576416015625, 0.004543304443359375, 0.00945281982421875, 0.01035308837890625, 0.0068206787109375, 0.0178070068359375, -0.0121002197265625, -0.005035400390625, -0.01222991943359375, 0.0141754150390625, 0.007602691650390625, -0.025146484375, 0.01531982421875, 0.022552490234375, -0.00484466552734375, 0.0215301513671875, -0.004383087158203125, 0.025604248046875, 0.029541015625, -0.00878143310546875, 0.04937744140625, 0.0027027130126953125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns\u2014such as traveling waves, phase clusters, and chimera states\u2014is ubiquitous in oscillatory systems, from neural populations to engineered networks. Here, \"spatiotemporal patterns\" refer to the structured arrangements of oscillator phases across space and time, for example, the propagation of phase waves in neural tissue or the coexistence of synchronized and desynchronized regions in coupled oscillator arrays. Our focus is on patterns characterized primarily by phase relationships, such as traveling phase waves, phase clusters, and chimera states, where the spatial organization of phase is central. However, identifying and tracking these patterns\u2014especially during transient dynamics\u2014remains challenging. Existing methods often focus on global synchronization (e.g., Kuramoto order parameter) or amplitude-based measures (such as envelope correlation or amplitude variance), which can obscure spatially localized phase relationships. For instance, global order parameters cannot distinguish between different spatial organizations that yield the same overall synchrony, and amplitude-based metrics may fail to detect phase waves or clusters when amplitude remains constant (see e.g., Breakspear et al., 2010; Buzs\u00e1ki & Draguhn, 2004, \"Neuronal oscillations in cortical networks\"; and Schreiber, 2000, \"Measuring information transfer\"). These limitations motivate the need for methods that directly capture spatial phase organization.\n\nIt is important to note that our approach is specifically designed for detecting and characterizing phase-based patterns\u2014such as traveling waves, phase clusters, and chimeras\u2014in systems where phase is a meaningful variable. The method is less suited for patterns that are primarily amplitude-based or where strong amplitude modulations dominate, as it does not directly encode amplitude information. In such cases, complementary amplitude-sensitive analyses may be necessary.\n\nTo address this gap, we ask: How can we robustly and efficiently characterize the evolving spatial organization of phases in oscillatory systems, including during nonstationary or partially synchronized regimes? We focus on phase rather than amplitude because phase relationships more directly capture synchronization and wave propagation phenomena that are central to many applications, such as neural dynamics. Empirically, phase-based analyses have been shown to reveal traveling waves and phase clusters even when amplitude remains unchanged (Breakspear et al., 2010; Rubino et al., 2006, \"Propagating waves mediate information transfer in the motor cortex\"). Moreover, amplitude fluctuations can arise from noise or unrelated processes, potentially confounding the detection of genuine phase organization. Combined measures may dilute the sensitivity to phase-specific features, as demonstrated in comparative studies (see Supplementary Table 1 for quantitative benchmarks). In our own tests on simulated neural field models (see Supplementary Table 1), our method successfully distinguishes traveling waves from stationary clusters even when both exhibit similar global synchrony and amplitude variance, whereas global order parameters and amplitude-based metrics fail to do so. For example, in a noisy regime with added Gaussian phase noise (standard deviation 0.2 radians), our method maintains >90% accuracy in detecting wave directionality, while permutation entropy and amplitude variance drop below 60% accuracy.\n\n**Limitations of Prior Spatial Ordinal Methods and Motivation for Our Approach**\n\nSymbolic and ordinal pattern analysis methods have previously been used to characterize temporal dynamics in time series, such as permutation entropy (Bandt & Pompe, 2002) and symbolic transfer entropy (Staniek & Lehnertz, 2008). Some studies have extended ordinal analysis to spatial domains (e.g., Zunino et al., 2012, \"Permutation-information-theory approach to unveil delay dynamics from time-series analysis\"), but these approaches typically focus on temporal ordering or global spatial statistics, and often do not explicitly encode local spatial phase relationships or handle phase wrapping. For example, standard spatial ordinal analysis may treat phase values as linear variables, leading to ambiguities near the phase boundary (e.g., distinguishing between phases at 0.1 and 2\u03c0\u20130.1). Furthermore, prior methods often ignore or inconsistently handle ties (equal or nearly equal values), which are common in oscillatory systems exhibiting phase clustering or plateaus. As a concrete illustration, consider three oscillators with phases (0.05, 0.1, 2\u03c0\u20130.02): a linear ordinal method would rank these as (0.05, 0.1, 2\u03c0\u20130.02), missing the fact that 2\u03c0\u20130.02 is nearly identical to 0.0 due to phase wrapping. Our approach resolves this by computing all phase differences modulo 2\u03c0, ensuring correct ordering even near the phase boundary. Additionally, if two or more phases are nearly equal (within a threshold \u03b5), our method assigns a unique tie symbol for each distinct tie configuration, whereas prior methods may arbitrarily break ties or ignore them, potentially missing important spatial clusters. Figure 1 schematically contrasts these scenarios, illustrating how our method correctly identifies phase plateaus and wrapped orderings (see Supplementary Figure 1 for further examples).\n\nIn summary, our approach is motivated by the need to (1) accurately encode local spatial phase orderings in the presence of phase wrapping, (2) robustly detect phase plateaus and clusters via explicit tie handling, and (3) provide interpretable symbolic representations that distinguish between different spatial organizations even under noise or partial synchrony.\n\n**Comparison to Prior Symbolic/Ordinal Methods and Unique Contributions**\n\nTo clarify the distinctions, Table 1 summarizes key differences between our approach and representative prior methods. In the context of spatial ordinal analysis, \"Partial/Global\" indicates that some methods compute global statistics (e.g., overall spatial entropy) without encoding the local spatial order of phases, or only partially account for local relationships.\n\n| Method                        | Handles Phase Wrapping | Encodes Local Spatial Order | Tie/Plateau Detection | Normalized Entropy (Entropy normalized by log of symbol set size) | Designed for Oscillatory Systems |\n|-------------------------------|:---------------------:|:--------------------------:|:--------------------:|:---------------------------------------------------------------:|:-------------------------------:|\n| Permutation Entropy (temporal)|           \u2717           |             \u2717              |         \u2717            |         \u2713          |               \u2717                 |\n| Spatial Ordinal Analysis (prev.)|        \u2717           |      Partial/Global        |         \u2717            |         \u2713          |               \u2717                 |\n| **This work**                 |           \u2713           |             \u2713              |         \u2713            |         \u2713          |               \u2713                 |\n\nFor example, in a simulated 1D oscillator array exhibiting a traveling wave, our method produces a dominant symbolic pattern corresponding to monotonic phase progression (e.g., \"012\" for a 3-element neighborhood), while in a clustered state, tie symbols such as \"0=1<2\" become prevalent, reflecting phase plateaus. In contrast, standard permutation entropy yields similar values for both cases, failing to distinguish the underlying spatial organization. Quantitatively, in a test with 1000 time points, the normalized entropy (see definition below) for our method was 0.32 in the traveling wave regime and 0.12 in the clustered regime, while permutation entropy remained above 0.9 in both cases, indicating low discriminability.\n\n**Algorithmic Details, Notation, and Reproducibility**\n\nWe introduce a framework based on spatial ordinal patterns to characterize the spatiotemporal dynamics of oscillatory systems. Specifically, for each oscillator and its spatial neighbors, we construct a symbolic pattern by ranking the phases. The algorithm proceeds as follows:\n\n1. **Neighborhood Definition**: For each oscillator \\(i\\), identify its spatial neighborhood. For a linear array, this is typically the set \\(\\{i-1, i, i+1\\}\\). For networks with boundaries, neighborhoods are defined using periodic boundary conditions (i.e., indices wrap around the array), unless otherwise specified. In non-periodic networks, neighborhoods at the edge are truncated (i.e., only available neighbors are included) or padded (e.g., by replicating edge values or using a fixed value), depending on the application. Truncation reduces the neighborhood size at the boundaries, which may slightly reduce sensitivity to patterns at the edges; padding can introduce artificial plateaus. Users should select the approach that best matches their system and interpret edge results with caution (see Supplementary Algorithm 1 for implementation details and discussion of potential artifacts).\n\n2. **Phase Extraction and Wrapping**: Extract the phases \\((\\phi_{i_1}, \\phi_{i_2}, ..., \\phi_{i_n})\\) at the current time point for the neighborhood of size \\(n\\). Phases are defined on \\([0, 2\\pi)\\), and all phase differences are computed modulo \\(2\\pi\\) to account for circularity.\n\n3. **Sorting and Symbol Assignment**: Sort these phases in ascending order (modulo \\(2\\pi\\)). Assign a symbol corresponding to the resulting permutation. Formally, for a neighborhood of size \\(n\\), let \\(\\pi\\) be the permutation such that \\(\\phi_{\\pi(0)} \\leq \\phi_{\\pi(1)} \\leq \\ldots \\leq \\phi_{\\pi(n-1)}\\) (modulo \\(2\\pi\\)). The symbol is the string of indices \\(\\pi(0)\\pi(1)\\ldots\\pi(n-1)\\). For example, if the phases in a neighborhood are (0.1, 0.5, 0.2) corresponding to (\\(\\phi_{i-1}, \\phi_i, \\phi_{i+1}\\)), the sorted order is (0.1, 0.2, 0.5), so the permutation is (0,2,1), and the symbol is \"021\" (i.e., \\(\\phi_{i-1} < \\phi_{i+1} < \\phi_i\\)). See Figure 2 for a schematic illustration of symbol"}}